{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And they assembled all the congregation on the first day of the second month</b> – Scripture mentions the alacrity of Moses our teacher in [fulfilling] the commandment of HaShem, for on the day of the speaking he took the princes and assembled the whole congregation and began to count them, but the count was not completed in a single day. Therefore he went back and said: \"And he counted them in the wilderness of Sinai\" (Numbers 1:19), to inform that the count took place in that place, not on that day." "<b>And they declared their pedigrees</b> – They brought their books of lineage and witnesses establishing the birth of each and every one, to be registered by tribe — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 1:18). But it does not seem that they would need to bring documents and witnesses for their lineage to their tribes. Rather, <b>וַיִּתְיַלְדוּ</b> – their descendants were counted by their families, by their fathers' houses. The meaning is to say that when Moses commanded them thus from the mouth of the Almighty, the entire congregation assembled, all of them, to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, except for the mixed multitude who were not of the children of Israel, for they were recognizable among them from the day they came out of Egypt. And so the whole congregation brought each man his shekel, and they said before Moses and the princes: \"I am So-and-so, born to So-and-so, of the family of So-and-so, which is of the tribe of Reuben,\" and so on, and Moses would set aside the shekels of each and every tribe in a designated place and know the particular number and the general number. And the proof of this is that in the second count, where he mentioned the families, it does not say there \"וַיִּתְיַלְדוּ,\" nor does it mention \"by the number of their names, by their polls,\" for from the time they encamped by their standards the tribes were distinguished one from another and the families became known as to which tribe they belonged, and there was no need there except to know how many families were in the tribe, and the family would count its men by their polls, not by the number of their names. Therefore there was no need for princes to be present at that count. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 1:18), <b>וַיִּתְיַלְדוּ</b> – they sought when they were born, for the purpose of calculating [those of] twenty years."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "The lemma reflects only the first of two quoted phrases in the Hebrew headword; the second, בְּאֶחָד לַחֹדֶשׁ הַשֵּׁנִי, is included to complete the sense.", 1 "הקב״ה resolved as 'the Almighty' in keeping with the rule that divine names are never rendered as English common nouns; this specific abbreviation (הקדוש ברוך הוא) is not among the listed standard transliterations but follows the same register."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:20.280025804Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}